Subject: Gambatte: answer 00, not FF, when the peer is there but not listening (v5irh)
Series-Step: 3/5 (gambatte link v5irh3)
Origin: hanbang (this project); record name hanbang_gb_v5irh_from_v5ir.diff
요약: 듣지 않는 상대에게 보낸 바이트(엇갈림 · 듣지 않음)에 FF 대신 00 으로 답함 — 실제 케이블처럼. 햄스터 파라다이스 햄 레이스가 "케이블 없음"으로 끊기던 것.

A Game Boy that is there but not shifting gives 0 bits on a cable (only a missing Game Boy reads FF). Hamster
Paradise starts both Game Boys as masters sending FA every frame and drops the race on an FF ("cable not connected"),
while Monster Capsule GB only looks for $61/$62 here, so 00 does what FF did for it. Applies to the host-first
resolution of crossed bytes (client side) and to the "not listening" answer.

---
diff --git a/libgambatte/libretro/hb_netpacket_serial.h b/libgambatte/libretro/hb_netpacket_serial.h
index de00377e2484..9186a7e416d7 100644
--- a/libgambatte/libretro/hb_netpacket_serial.h
+++ b/libgambatte/libretro/hb_netpacket_serial.h
@@ -413,10 +413,15 @@ public:
                 * the client's byte for when it listens. (v4bv gave each the other's byte, and
                 * two Game Boys retrying a call on the same beat crossed every time: Monster
                 * Capsule GB '통신 준비가 되어 있지 않습니다'.) */
+               /* v5irh: 00, not FF: the client had not started yet and a Game Boy whose port
+                * is off gives 0 bits on a cable (SameBoy's link model; FF is only for no Game
+                * Boy at all). Hamster Paradise starts both Game Boys as masters sending FA every
+                * frame and drops the race on an FF ('케이블이 연결되어 있지 않습니다'); Monster
+                * Capsule GB only looks for $61 here, so 00 does what FF did. */
                if (!is_host_) {
-                  put('R', u.seq, 0xFF, 128, 0);
+                  put('R', u.seq, 0x00, 128, 0);
                   u.type = 0;
-                  tr("crossQ-ff", u.seq, u.data, seq);
+                  tr("crossQ-00", u.seq, u.data, seq);
                } else if (!crossed) {
                   crossed = true;
                   tr("crossQ-keep", u.seq, u.data, seq);
@@ -626,7 +631,11 @@ private:
    void drop_used() { while (head_ != tail_ && q_[head_].type != 'Q') head_ = (head_ + 1) & (HB_NP_QUEUE - 1); }
    /* v4bw: a byte the other Game Boy clocked out while this one is not listening (SC not armed
     * for an external clock) gets FF back once this side ran HB_NP_UNARMED cycles past when it
-    * was due (or first seen), as on a cable, instead of holding the sender up to HB_NP_TIMEOUT_MS. */
+    * was due (or first seen), as on a cable, instead of holding the sender up to HB_NP_TIMEOUT_MS.
+    * v5irh: 00 instead of FF: a Game Boy that is there but not shifting gives 0 bits (SameBoy's
+    * link model; only a missing one reads FF, see send()). Hamster Paradise's master took the FF
+    * for '케이블이 연결되어 있지 않습니다' while the other one still waited its random 8 to 39
+    * frames or had just switched back to master. Monster Capsule GB compares with $61/$62 only. */
    void unarmed(unsigned long cycles, unsigned char sc)
    {
       if (!active())
@@ -648,8 +657,8 @@ private:
       }
       if (cycles >= unarmed_from_ && cycles - unarmed_from_ >= HB_NP_UNARMED) {
          clock_ = cycles;
-         put('R', u.seq, 0xFF, 128, 0);
-         tr("unarmedFF", u.seq, u.data, (unsigned)(cycles - unarmed_from_));
+         put('R', u.seq, 0x00, 128, 0);
+         tr("unarmed00", u.seq, u.data, (unsigned)(cycles - unarmed_from_));
          head_ = (head_ + 1) & (HB_NP_QUEUE - 1);
          unarmed_seq_ = 0x10000;
       }
